// Test: a sibling written INSIDE the shadowed field is rejected too.
//
// `exec_time_s2c_magic_fragment_corridor_rejects_sibling.invalid` covers a
// sibling one level up, beside the shadowed field. This is the other placement,
// and it is the one that occurs in real code:
//
//     me {
//       title              # author-written, nothing fetches it
//       ...ReturnFragment  # becomes @__relay_shadow_return on `me`
//     }
//
// The placeholder is converted into a directive on the ENCLOSING field, so after
// conversion both the author's `title` and the injected `id`/`__typename` are
// children of a marker-bearing `me`. Skipping a marker-bearing subtree therefore
// skipped the author's sibling as well, and `title` — which the transplant does
// NOT fetch, since the transplant carries the CONSUMER's selections — read back
// undefined at exec time.
//
// Found in `AdsFrameworksLabWeakShadowResolver`, whose root fragment is exactly
// this shape and which compiled clean when it should not have.
//- Consumer.js
graphql`query ConsumerQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  shadow_page {
    title
  }
}`

